module extdeps.physics.double_slit

import std.types { Bool, NonEmptyStr, String }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalAuthority }
import extdeps.uri { Uri, Https }

data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "iopscience.iop.org/article/10.1088/1367-2630/15/3/033018"
  }
}

data double_slit_citation_note: String = "Cited upstream: Bach, R., Pope, D., Liou, S.-H. and Batelaan, H., 'Controlled double-slit electron diffraction', New Journal of Physics volume 15, article 033018, 2013 (doi 10.1088/1367-2630/15/3/033018). Retrieved and verified 2026-08-03: title, author list, journal, volume, article number and year were read from the publisher record rather than recalled.\n\nWHY THIS PAPER AND NOT A MORE FAMOUS ONE. The contradiction this module exists to expose needs the single-slit distributions and the double-slit distribution measured on the SAME apparatus, because the classical prediction is a statement relating them. Joensson 1961 and Tonomura 1989 are the celebrated electron double-slit results, but this experiment is the one with a movable mask that opens and closes individual slits without rebuilding the apparatus, so its single-slit and double-slit rows are comparable by construction rather than across runs. Verbatim from the abstract: 'By placing a movable mask in front of a double-slit to control the transmission through the individual slits, probability distributions for single- and double-slit arrangements were observed.' The publisher record further describes the mask as giving a non-destructive way to reach these configurations, in contrast to earlier work where a slit was permanently closed.\n\nWHAT THIS MODULE IS AND IS NOT. It carries the APPARATUS VOCABULARY and the OBSERVED QUALITATIVE OUTCOMES of this one experiment. It carries no classical reasoning: the premises that make the observation paradoxical are not this paper's claims, and modelling them here would give one module two independently governed subjects, which DESIGN section 3's external-upstream decomposition forbids. That reasoning is a downstream product-layer fold over these rows.\n\nNO FABRICATED INTENSITIES. The paper reports measured probability distributions; this module does NOT transcribe a numeric intensity profile, because a curve recalled rather than read would be exactly the fabricated-plausible-output failure DESIGN section 5 forbids, and no oracle here could catch it. What is modelled is the qualitative structure the distributions exhibit, which is what the logical contradiction actually turns on. A numeric row lands only behind a real read of the paper's figures, as its own typed addition."

data which_path_detection_gap_note: String = "TYPED GAP, NOT AN OMISSION TO BE QUIETLY FILLED. The 'photons on or off' axis -- illuminating the slits so that the path taken becomes knowable, and watching the fringes disappear -- is NOT part of this experiment and must not be attributed to it. Bach et al. controlled which SLITS were open, not whether WHICH-PATH information existed. Modelling a which-path row inside this module would put a claim in a cited authority's mouth that the authority never made, which is the stale-citation class one step worse: not a citation that rotted, but one that was wrong on arrival.\n\nThe which-path axis is a SEPARATE SUBJECT and needs its own extdeps module with its own citation before any consumer may fold over it. Until that lands, a consumer asking this module what happens under which-path detection gets WhichPathOutcomeUncited -- a refusal naming the missing authority, never a guess. OWNER: the quantum-mechanics lane. DISSOLVE-ON: a cited which-path or quantum-eraser experiment module existing, at which point the refusal arm is replaced by that module's rows and this note is deleted."

data slit_arrangement_note: String = "The mask positions are the experiment's own control axis: transmission through the individual slits. BothSlitsOpen and the two single-slit arrangements are the three the paper reports. BothSlitsBlocked is included because the classical prediction is only meaningful against a measured zero -- a partition argument that never checks the empty configuration cannot tell 'no electrons arrived' from 'the apparatus was not running'."

type SlitArrangement
  = BothSlitsOpen
  | OnlyLeftSlitOpen
  | OnlyRightSlitOpen
  | BothSlitsBlocked

data screen_band_note: String = "Detector-screen positions are modelled as the three LOGICALLY DISTINGUISHED bands, not as a coordinate. A coordinate would demand a fitted curve this module refuses to invent; the bands are the positions at which the classical prediction and the observation actually disagree, which is all the contradiction needs. CentralBand is the axis of symmetry; FirstMinimumBand is the first position where the double-slit distribution has a minimum; FirstSideMaximumBand is the first maximum away from the axis. Under a single open slit these are ordinary positions with no special status, which is itself the point -- the structure exists only when both slits are open."

type ScreenBand
  = CentralBand
  | FirstMinimumBand
  | FirstSideMaximumBand

data fringe_structure_note: String = "The observed qualitative outcome per arrangement, and the strongest thing this module states. FringesPresent means the distribution exhibits interference structure -- alternating maxima and minima -- across the screen. FringesAbsent means it does not: a single broad distribution. NoArrivals is its own arm rather than a degenerate FringesAbsent, because 'the pattern has no fringes' and 'nothing was detected' are different observations and collapsing them would be the state-space conflation DESIGN's failure-mode list names."

type FringeStructure
  = FringesPresent
  | FringesAbsent
  | NoArrivals

fn observed_fringe_structure(arrangement: SlitArrangement) -> FringeStructure {
  match arrangement {
    BothSlitsOpen => FringesPresent
    OnlyLeftSlitOpen => FringesAbsent
    OnlyRightSlitOpen => FringesAbsent
    BothSlitsBlocked => NoArrivals
  }
}

data second_slit_relation_note: String = "THE ONE RELATION THAT CARRIES THE CONTRADICTION, stated as a comparison rather than as two numbers. At the first minimum of the double-slit distribution, MORE electrons arrive with a single slit open than with both slits open. That is the whole paradox in one sentence, and it is a SIGN, not a magnitude -- which is why it can be modelled honestly without transcribing an intensity curve.\n\nStated as a relation it is also directly falsifiable by a later reader with the figures in hand: it says the double-slit count at FirstMinimumBand is strictly below the single-slit count at that same band, and nothing more."

type BandComparison
  = BothOpenExceedsSingle
  | BothOpenEqualsSingle
  | BothOpenBelowSingle

fn observed_both_versus_single(band: ScreenBand) -> BandComparison {
  match band {
    CentralBand => BothOpenExceedsSingle
    FirstMinimumBand => BothOpenBelowSingle
    FirstSideMaximumBand => BothOpenExceedsSingle
  }
}

data single_electron_note: String = "Detection is one electron at a time; the distribution accumulates from individual arrivals rather than appearing at once. This matters to the logical model downstream because it removes the escape that electrons interfere with EACH OTHER: whatever produces the structure is available to a single electron traversing the apparatus alone."

fn arrivals_are_individually_detected() -> Bool {
  true
}

data which_path_refusal_note: String = "The fail-closed arm for the uncited axis above. A consumer that asks this module for a which-path outcome does not get a plausible answer; it gets the name of the authority that would have to exist first."

type WhichPathOutcome
  = WhichPathOutcomeUncited { missing_authority: NonEmptyStr }

fn observed_under_which_path_detection() -> WhichPathOutcome {
  WhichPathOutcomeUncited {
    missing_authority:
      "a cited which-path or quantum-eraser experiment; extdeps.physics.double_slit models Bach et al. 2013, which controlled slit transmission and not path knowledge"
      as NonEmptyStr,
  }
}
